delf of a function while its thread is running.

The realtime thread walks its funct_list without the HAL mutex. Before
the fix, hal_del_funct_from_thread() unlinked the entry (its links then
pointed to itself) and recycled it at once; a thread that was on the
entry kept calling it or followed a recycled link, and a following
unloadrt dlclose()d code the thread could still execute.

Now delf (ULAPI) waits until the thread has left the removed entry
(threadbeat advanced by two), at most 1000 thread periods; on timeout it
fails and the entry is not recycled, so the function stays in use.
halcmd unloadrt asks hal_comp_check_unload() first, which refuses under
the HAL mutex while a function of the component is in a running thread.
If unloading still reaches hal_exit() (RTAPI, no waiting there) with the
threads running, the entry is unlinked, not recycled, and an error is
reported. The supported way to replace a component in a running thread
is delf first, then unloadrt.

delftest.py makes the thread stay inside the function of delfvictim
(pin hold, 500 periods) and runs delf from another process. When delf
returns, pin inside must be false. Before the delf, unloadrt must
fail; after it, unloadrt must work and t.threadbeat must keep advancing.
Last, the usual stop, unloadrt, start sequence is checked. Pins are
accessed with hal.set_p() and hal.get_p(); there are no nets. A wait
that makes no progress for 10 s aborts the test.
